Fix silent loss of non-ASCII letters (AE, OE, AA, accented Latin) in
generated URIs.

URIGenerator.normalize(String) strips characters with
Pattern.compile("[^\w -]+") BEFORE calling
Normalizer.normalize(formattedSrc, Normalizer.Form.NFD) at the end of the
method. Java's \w only matches ASCII word characters, so every non-ASCII
letter -- not just symbols -- is deleted by the strip regex before the
trailing NFD call ever runs. That trailing call is dead code: it
decomposes a string that has already lost the characters NFD decomposition
would have helped with.

Confirmed repro (GitHub issue #7): normalize("test_ÆØÅ_[-1:2]") currently
returns "test__-12" -- Æ, Ø and Å vanish silently along with the bracket/
colon symbols, producing an unpredictable, information-lossy URI.

Fix: perform NFD normalization and combining-mark stripping (\p{Mn}) up
front, on the raw input, so accented Latin letters (é, ü, ö, ...) degrade
to their ASCII base letter instead of being deleted by the later strip.
Æ/Ø/Å don't decompose under NFD (they're atomic letters, not a base plus
a combining mark), so they get an explicit transliteration per the issue's
own suggestion: Æ/æ -> ae, Ø/ø -> oe, Å/å -> aa (normalize() lowercases
everything anyway, so mapping straight to the lowercase form is enough).

This does not change the existing, intentionally-tested symbol-stripping
behavior (URIGeneratorTest.testNormalization asserts symbols like $ & ~
" ' { } ( ) [ ] | ` \ ^ @ = + % * ! : / ; . , ? are removed entirely) --
that part of the method and its test both keep passing unchanged. Only
letters that were being silently destroyed are now preserved.

